working_notes/lemma_bank.md:313:Known gaps: The final paper must state the admissible measurable policy class and prove that arbitrary private-randomized policies reduce to prefix marginal kernels because prices are exogenous and rewards are additive. Continuum quotes still require the separate \(T/K\) grid comparison.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:316:## Lemma: Payoff-weighted reward-measure domination no-gap theorem
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:341:Each feasible set \(Q_t=L^\infty(\lambda_t;\Delta(G))\) is weak-* compact: boundedness follows from the simplex constraint, positivity is weak-* closed by testing against \(L^1_+\), and \(\sum_a q_{t,a}=1\) is weak-* closed by testing against \(L^1\). The finite product \(Q=\prod_tQ_t\) is compact, and each coordinate \(q\mapsto L_\theta(q)\) is weak-* continuous. Thus \(C\), the continuous image into the product space \([0,T]^\Theta\), is product compact. Apply the product-closed no-gap criterion and the finite-class grid minimax/Bayes identity on finite \(F\).
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:342:Known gaps: For the original policy class, still prove that arbitrary private-randomized policies with memory and delayed feedback reduce to law-independent prefix marginal kernels under the exact admissible model. If original policies are required to be Borel, add a Borel-version/completed-measure implementation lemma for \(L^\infty\) kernels and use the same policy class for finite-subclass values and the full-class value. This is fixed-grid only.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:364:Known gaps: The final write-up must identify this density expression with the earlier conditional-probability Bayes envelope \(\sum_t\mathbb E_{\bar P_\pi}\max_{a\in G}a\,\bar P_\pi(M_t>a\mid P_{1:t})\). This should follow because the mixture payoff-weighted density for arm \(a\) is \(\sum_\theta\pi_\theta f^\theta_{t,a}\).
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:372:Known gaps: Weak or Feller continuity alone does not imply total-variation separability or domination. Uncountable Dirac families are the basic stress test.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:375:## Lemma: Dominated approximation gives approximate no-gap
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:381:Statement candidate: The full infinite-class grid value and the finite-subclass modulus differ by at most \(O(\varepsilon)\), and exact no-gap follows if \(\varepsilon\to0\).
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:382:Proof sketch: Each regret coordinate \(V_\theta^G-L_\theta(q)\) changes by at most about \(2\varepsilon\), both for the full class and for every finite subclass. Apply the exact no-gap theorem to the dominated surrogate and transfer back. Constants still need to be written carefully.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:383:Known gaps: The premise is strong; uniform approximation over all Borel policies is essentially total-variation-like. Keep as a candidate corollary until constants and policy-class alignment are checked.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:389:Statement candidate: A single public-prefix policy can decode \(\theta\) after the first observation and run the known-law grid oracle for that \(\theta\), yielding \(O(T/K)\) or nonpositive grid regret depending on how the first round is handled, despite mutually singular prefix laws.
working_notes/lemma_bank.md:390:Known gaps: This is not a compactness/product-closedness theorem and needs measurable dependence assumptions. Non-Borel label examples show why those assumptions cannot be omitted.
