{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"Could fail if the policy model permits law-by-law a.s. policy equivalence, private feedback not reconstructible from public prices, or internal state not representable by independent seeds. The result is only reward-vector equivalence, not pathwise equivalence.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","measurability audit"],"summary":"Write an exact finite-grid public-exogenous admissible policy model and prove the forward alignment lemma: every common Borel private-randomized policy induces law-independent Borel public-prefix kernels q_t(P_{1:t}) preserving all expected reward coordinates L_theta. Include persistent internal state and delayed trade feedback explicitly.","title":"Borel Policy Alignment","why_promising":"This is the main remaining gap between the accepted completed-L-infinity no-gap theorem and a theorem for the intended original policy class. It is local, finite-horizon, and should be checkable by induction plus Fubini."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Null sets under the mixture prefix law may obscure the equality if the dominating measure is chosen poorly. Need handle zero-density prefixes and verify finite-G measurability cleanly.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","measure-theory verification"],"summary":"Prove rigorously that the density-form support formula under payoff-measure domination equals the conditional Bayes envelope defining Psi_{G,F}. Work with finite F, prior pi, measures nu_{t,a}=sum_theta pi_theta mu^theta_{t,a}, and compare Radon-Nikodym densities using both lambda_t and the mixture prefix law.","title":"Density Psi Identification","why_promising":"The iteration 12 solver asserts this as a concrete new closure step. A short proof would make the infinite-class no-gap statement use the same Psi notation as the finite-class theorem."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A counterexample may require pathological definitions already excluded by a careful policy model. Explorers should distinguish genuine failures from failures caused by changing the model.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","model audit"],"summary":"Stress-test the claimed original-policy alignment by varying assumptions one at a time: private/censored feedback, action-dependent prices, law-correlated randomization, policies defined only up to law-specific null sets, or same-period trading. For each variant, either produce a counterexample or explain why the original assumptions exclude it.","title":"Alignment Failure Modes","why_promising":"This will prevent the final theorem from silently relying on hidden assumptions. It can also clarify exactly why public exogeneity and common Borel policies are essential."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Naive block repetition collapses because the fixed comparator sees expected mixtures, and one reveal can teach the global type. Need avoid these known failures explicitly.","suggested_lenses":["lower-bound construction","Bayes-gap calculation"],"summary":"Design multi-scale or limited-leakage finite-horizon classes that accumulate many hidden-terminal Bayes gaps while satisfying a tail envelope B(h), aiming to match or approach the upper rate sqrt(T H)+T B(H). Candidate designs: independent hidden bits at separated quote levels, terminal observations that reveal only noisy summaries, or Fano-style families with small KL across public prefixes.","title":"Power-Tail Lower Rates","why_promising":"Matching lower rates are the largest open quantitative issue. The accepted finite-grid Bayes value formula gives a way to compute or certify lower bounds for carefully designed finite classes."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Running many H-window algorithms may incur too much overhead, and delayed feedback timing may break standard model-selection arguments. Comparator-gap terms may not combine in a clean oracle inequality.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","upper-bound proof"],"summary":"Investigate an adaptive truncation algorithm competing with the best truncation horizon H in hindsight, targeting an oracle bound like inf_H {sqrt(T H log K)+T/K+delta_T(H)} plus logarithmic overhead. Try dyadic H, meta-Hedge over base delayed-expert algorithms, and careful accounting of when each base algorithm receives feedback.","title":"Adaptive Truncation","why_promising":"The current sufficient theorem requires choosing H from a known tail profile. Adaptivity would strengthen the upper theory and may reveal whether the present rates are proof artifacts."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"May either reduce to domination/TV separability or to easy identifiability. Need avoid claiming weak/Feller continuity alone, which is already known insufficient.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Search for natural no-gap hypotheses beyond payoff-measure domination: finite-time Borel identifiability, dominated-on-fibers after public labels, analytic parametric families with measurable decoders, or direct compactness of the attainable reward set. State checkable sufficient conditions and test them against uncountable Dirac examples.","title":"Natural Infinite-Class No-Gap","why_promising":"Common payoff-measure domination excludes many deterministic or singular classes. A broader no-gap theorem would make the Bayes-value characterization substantially more useful."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Known examples are pathological non-Borel label constructions. A natural Borel gap may not exist under standard public-prefix policies, or may require a more subtle descriptive-set-theoretic setup.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","descriptive-set-theory audit"],"summary":"Try to construct a Borel, nondominated, non-identifiable infinite class where every finite subclass has small grid minimax value but the full Borel-policy class has linear value. Alternatively, prove that broad Borel/analytic regularity plus finite-grid public exogeneity rules out such gaps under an appropriate measurability condition.","title":"Borel Infinite-Class 